<script>
import Forecast from 'crm/src/reports/lib/Forecast.abide'
import forecastSource from 'crm/src/reports/lib/Forecast.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import forecastBenchSource from 'crm/src/reports/lib/Forecast.bench.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import forecastTestSource from 'crm/src/reports/lib/Forecast.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import forecastLogicSource from 'crm/src/reports/lib/forecast.ts' with { type: 'text' }

import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="A bench prices code at two sizes">
        <Example
            id="forecast-bench"
            app="crm"
            title="`batch(spec)` — one op, timed at two sizes"
            summary="The forecast weights every deal by the odds of its stage. Advance a deal and the forecast moves; the bench times that among 100 deals and among 1,000."
            files={{
                'src/reports/lib/Forecast.abide': forecastSource,
                'src/reports/lib/forecast.ts': forecastLogicSource,
                'src/reports/lib/Forecast.test.ts': forecastTestSource,
                'src/reports/lib/Forecast.bench.ts': forecastBenchSource,
            }}
        >
            <Forecast />
        </Example>

        <p>A millisecond describes the machine it was taken on. A ratio between two sizes of the
            same structure says how the cost grows, and that holds on any machine. Near 1, the op costs
            the same however many deals there are. Near 10, it grows with the deals: the forecast reads
            every one of them.</p>

        <p><code>batch</code> times every arm in one run, interleaved, and sizes each sample to a
            hundred times what the clock can resolve. It runs the first arm against itself too, and
            the spread between those two runs is the floor. <code>ratio</code> reports a difference
            inside the floor as a tag rather than a number, and <code>claimOf</code> is the one line a
            report prints.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the CRM</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>BatchSpec</code></td><td>the arms, the case's name, and whether the op touches an emulated DOM</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Batched</code></td><td>what <code>batch</code> hands back: a <code>Sample</code> per arm, and the floor</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Sample</code></td><td>one arm's time per op, its batch size, and whether it was <code>underOneFrame</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ratio</code> · <code>RatioSpec</code> · <code>Ratio</code></td><td>one sample over another, judged against the floor</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>claimOf</code></td><td>a <code>Ratio</code> as one printed line</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>emulatedDom</code></td><td>whether this process's DOM is an emulator</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>time</code></td><td><code>batch</code> with <code>emulated</code> filled in, refusing to run while the counters are armed</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>THRESHOLDS</code></td><td>a frame and an interaction, the two lines a cost is judged against</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>runBenches</code></td><td>runs every bench under <code>src</code>, and reports each arm over its ceiling</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>CEILINGS</code></td><td>the most each arm of a bench may take, in milliseconds, by bench and then by arm</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>harness-bench</code></td><td>the <code>bench</code> script: <code>runBenches</code> printed, failing where a ceiling is not held</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>batch</code> throws on a duration whose op touched an emulated DOM, and on a sample
            taken in a parallel test worker. That is why the bench builds no component: it times the
            derived value the component reads. Run the benches with <code>bun run bench</code>, never
            inside <code>bun test</code>.</p>

        <p>The <code>bench</code> script is <code>harness-bench</code>, which hands
            <code>runBenches</code> every <code>*.bench.ts</code> under <code>src</code>, wherever one
            sits beside the code it times, and prints each
            arm's time and the claim between them. A bench file's <code>CEILINGS</code> names the most
            each arm may take, in milliseconds, and the run fails where an arm takes more.</p>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
